  be deciding your policies and your  strategies rather than yourselves well  let you know this through the work that  sky does Jeremy and you you have to go  where the audience's where voters are  and the national conversation is taking  much taking part is taking place online  and if Labour's not there then we're not  going to be able to do  and help shape that so we're both very  committed to reforming our own party to  make sure that we're a digital-first  party but these are you know that kind  of change in digitally talked about  there was also some traditions in there  as well you know at this conference the  two of us have been to 37 events where  we've been addressing members on all  sorts of issues and I think he's that  kind of politician he wants to connect  with the British people and he wants to  go and talk to them and listen to what  they've got to say he's a pluralist in a  Democrat and you know it dare I say a  great antidote to the politics of spin  that is probably afflicted politics for  about a decade more than it should have  done isn't there a danger though that by  being such a listening leader by  listening to the online community as  well he may be so democratic he fails to  make the decisions that are needed in  the end how do you marry that up well  it's early days it you know we've got a  fixed-term parliaments come out of an  election defeat he's only been in office  for a couple of weeks so I just say give  him a little bit of time let's help  shape a national conversation on some  issues that he feels very strongly about  and ask me that question in a couple of  years time Jeremy when we've gone out  and talk to the country okay a kinder  gentler more caring type of politics i'm  sure a lot of people would have poor  that but how does it work when if ennis  in essence Westminster is an adversarial  system well I think it's about  respecting the views that people hold  not sort of not dismissing what they  stand for and it was really 